Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ Here's a simple C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

/**
 * Converts markdown formatted text to html formatted text.
 *
 * @param markdownText The markdown formatted text to convert.
 * @return The html formatted text.
 */
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    // Replace markdown syntax with html syntax
    std::string htmlText = markdownText;
    size_t pos = 0;
    while ((pos = htmlText.find("[", pos)) != std::string::npos) {
        htmlText.replace(pos, 1, "<");
        pos += 1;
        while ((pos = htmlText.find("]", pos)) != std::string::npos) {
            htmlText.replace(pos, 1, ">");
            pos += 1;
        }
    }
    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "This is a [test](http://example.com) with [multiple](http://example2.com) links.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << "Markdown text: " << markdownText << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML text: " << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function works by replacing the markdown syntax with the corresponding html syntax. The markdown syntax is defined as `[text](url)`, where `text` is the text and `url` is the URL. The function uses two while loops to find all occurrences of `[` and `]` in the markdown text and replace them with `<` and `>`, respectively. The `main` function demonstrates how to use this function by converting a markdown text to html text.

